cartridge "VEE KLOROS/CLEVE BLAKEMORE/1988"

' VEE KLOROS
' A ColecoVision game based on "Vee Kloros" by Cleve Blakemore, Ahoy!
' March 1988 (Commodore 64, BASIC with a machine-language sprite driver,
' KLOROS.OBJ). Made for Amy Studio by Claude (an AI made by Anthropic),
' 2026, in Amy by Amy Bienvenu.
'
' The game: a fighter flies over V-KLS, the mystery planet, while the
' ground scrolls down beneath it. Two flying saucers bounce around the sky
' and one of them is always shooting at the fighter, aimed in one of eight
' directions. The stick flies the fighter, the button fires: a shot goes up
' and kills a saucer (10 points x stage); when the bomb sight (a square 80
' pixels ahead of the fighter) is over a dome or a gun turret on the
' ground, the button drops a bomb on it instead (50 points x stage). Every
' 500 miles the ground changes (oceans, desert, jungle, city 1, city 2,
' then round again), a flag is raised on the panel, the stage goes up and
' the saucers' shots get faster. Three ships; a saucer or a shot that
' touches the fighter costs one. The game goes on until the last ship.
'
' From the original (orig/, the BASIC and orig/obj.dis): the fighter
' moves a pixel a frame in eight directions; one shot at a time, 5 pixels a
' frame, fired again while the button is held; the sight rides 90 C64
' pixels above the fighter (here 80) and stops at the top; a press with the
' sight over a ground target bombs it instead of shooting; two saucers
' move diagonally a pixel a frame and bounce off the four sides of the
' playfield; a hit one blows up and comes back from the top; one enemy shot
' at a time, from a saucer picked at random, aimed by the signs of the
' x and y distances (eight directions), at a speed of 1, 1, 2, 3 by stage,
' gone at the playfield's edge; two ground targets (dome, turret) scroll
' down with the ground at a pixel every two frames and come back at random
' places; 10 x (stage) and 50 x (stage) points; 500 miles a terrain; the
' starting terrain chosen with the stick on the title; the panel (terrain,
' score, high, ships, flags, miles); 3 ships; the second saucer takes the
' jungle's and the cities' own shapes.
'
' Liberties: the screen is the ColecoVision's (playfield 168 x 192, panel
' on the right); the ground is a 16 x 16 picture per terrain as on the C64,
' drawn anew; the bomb falls for 10 frames to the target it was aimed at
' (the C64 blew it up at once); a crater can't be bombed again (the C64
' scored it again); the enemy shot's speed is capped at 3 pixels a frame
' (the C64 went on to 4, 5, 6... after the fifth terrain); an enemy shot
' aimed exactly at the fighter's place falls straight down (on the C64 it
' stood still and stopped all enemy fire); after a lost ship the fighter
' is safe for two seconds (blinking at 2.5 a second; on the C64 a saucer
' could be waiting at the restart point); the miles count a mile every 16
' frames (the C64 counted BASIC loop passes, about 3.7 a second); a ground
' target comes back after a random pause; the saucers spin more slowly
' than the C64's colour cycling. Sounds, title tune, credits are new.
'
' Controls: joystick only. The stick flies, either side button fires or
' bombs. On the title, left and right choose the starting terrain, a
' button starts.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off
' after a wait, display off. Music first in the sound table, effects after;
' the title tune is played once. `set sprite count` after every `clear
' sprites`. Own xorshift random numbers.

